WEBVTT

00:00.816 --> 00:03.831
Collection requested.
Equipment still on site.

00:03.931 --> 00:05.843
Nepsis shows why a working demo

00:05.893 --> 00:07.678
is not a handoff—and what the

00:07.728 --> 00:09.441
team needs before takeover.

00:11.971 --> 00:13.159
The example is simple:

00:13.209 --> 00:14.737
a rented machine is ready to

00:14.787 --> 00:16.294
leave the site. On a perfect

00:16.344 --> 00:18.403
day, the supervisor marks it ready,

00:18.482 --> 00:21.376
the supplier is contacted,
the machine is collected,

00:21.426 --> 00:24.270
and the office receives
the expected confirmation.

00:24.413 --> 00:27.143
A clean demo can make
that route look finished.

00:29.673 --> 00:31.711
But the screen is not the workflow.

00:31.790 --> 00:33.951
The team first needs clear states.

00:34.094 --> 00:38.182
Ready for collection means site use has
finished. Collection requested means

00:38.232 --> 00:41.482
the supplier has been contacted.
Collected means the machine

00:41.560 --> 00:45.500
has physically left. Supplier confirmation
received is another state where

00:45.550 --> 00:48.913
the applicable process requires
it. The relevant hire terms and

00:48.963 --> 00:52.656
confirmations still need to be
checked; a button does not decide them.

00:55.186 --> 00:58.808
Each transition also needs
an expected action or source.

00:58.929 --> 01:00.472
Who marks the machine ready?

01:00.551 --> 01:02.541
What information goes to the supplier?

01:02.641 --> 01:03.999
Who can confirm it left?

01:04.049 --> 01:05.986
Where is the supplier reference kept?

01:06.074 --> 01:09.025
What happens if the normal
evidence does not arrive?

01:09.189 --> 01:11.144
Without those answers, one green

01:11.194 --> 01:13.761
status can hide several
different realities.

01:16.291 --> 01:19.071
Now the site supervisor
runs the normal case.

01:19.150 --> 01:21.655
The consultant does not
narrate every click.

01:21.705 --> 01:24.216
The supervisor identifies
the machine, records

01:24.266 --> 01:27.160
that it is ready, makes
or records the collection

01:27.210 --> 01:29.890
request, and watches the
office receive the correct

01:29.940 --> 01:33.334
state. The route works when the
people doing the job can follow it

01:33.384 --> 01:35.524
without being coached through every move.

01:38.054 --> 01:40.004
Then the consultant steps away.

01:40.106 --> 01:41.891
This is the retention reset and

01:41.941 --> 01:43.811
the real test: the builder is

01:43.861 --> 01:45.518
no longer in the room, and

01:45.568 --> 01:47.537
the user owns the next action.

01:50.067 --> 01:52.303
The supplier does not collect the machine.

01:52.519 --> 01:53.977
It is still in the yard,

01:54.055 --> 01:57.177
but the old workflow
offers one tempting word:

01:57.255 --> 01:59.808
done. Done could mean the request was

01:59.858 --> 02:02.197
sent. It could mean the machine left.

02:02.247 --> 02:04.314
It could mean a confirmation arrived.

02:04.448 --> 02:07.214
The exception exposes
an ambiguous state and

02:07.264 --> 02:09.489
no named person for the follow-up.

02:12.019 --> 02:15.852
The team corrects the workflow. The
status stays at collection requested

02:15.902 --> 02:17.729
while the machine remains on site.

02:17.779 --> 02:19.265
A named person owns the

02:19.315 --> 02:21.760
follow-up. The next check is visible.

02:21.810 --> 02:24.618
The supplier reference stays
attached. If collection

02:24.668 --> 02:28.843
fails again, the issue follows an agreed
escalation route instead of disappearing

02:28.893 --> 02:30.264
behind a completed button.

02:32.794 --> 02:34.781
Then the same exception is run again.

02:34.920 --> 02:36.612
The machine remains visible.

02:36.712 --> 02:38.553
The status remains accurate.

02:38.632 --> 02:40.430
The follow-up reaches its owner.

02:40.509 --> 02:43.353
The office can see what
happened and what comes next.

02:43.453 --> 02:44.470
Only after the user

02:44.520 --> 02:47.975
can complete that route does the
workflow move toward handoff.

02:50.505 --> 02:52.710
Handoff includes the operating note,

02:52.760 --> 02:54.885
the named owner, the normal path,

02:54.935 --> 02:57.275
the fallback, and the exception route. The

02:57.325 --> 03:00.276
consultant leaves the path;
the team keeps running it.

03:02.806 --> 03:04.122
A screen can pass a demo

03:04.172 --> 03:06.084
while the operation still fails.

03:06.233 --> 03:09.469
Nepsis tests the people,
states, decisions,

03:09.519 --> 03:12.967
and exceptions together. Bring
one workflow your team has

03:13.017 --> 03:17.270
to work around and start with the
free consultation and Basic Diagnosis.
